From 673c6083d08e8fcd0c3fa14211ed17c04395ec85 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexander Penev <alexander.p.penev@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 17:22:25 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] tests: the DistRender startup race, diagnosed and closed

DistRender.PushSchedulerCoversFrame timed out at 60 s under ctest -j16 on a
2-core box - one run in six - and took 0.07 s on its own. gdb on the hung
process settled what it was:

    Thread 2  Master::run -> TcpListener::accept -> poll(timeout=200)
    Thread 1  main, in std::thread::join()
    (both worker threads already exited)

So both workers had finished while the master was still waiting to accept
one. The master binds on its own thread and the test starts the workers
immediately; starved of CPU, a worker reaches connect() before the listener
exists. With retry_secs at its default 0 that connect fails at once, run()
returns the error, the test DISCARDED it, the worker exited - and the master
waited forever for a connection nobody would make.

The library already has the fix and the two later tests in this same file
already use it: WorkerConfig::retry_secs, whose comment reads "retry connect
for N s (LAN startup race)". The two older tests simply never set it. Both
now do, and neither discards w.run() any more - a worker that gives up
should read as a connect failure, not as a ctest timeout.

Verified where it failed: 30 runs of all DistRender tests under 8x CPU
oversubscription, then six full ctest -j16 passes. No hang, no failure.

Worth keeping: nothing here was a wall-clock assertion, and the earlier
guesses (TIME_WAIT, a port collision between tests) were both wrong -
SO_REUSEADDR is set and every test in the file has its own port. A
backtrace from the hung process cost less than any of the reasoning that
preceded it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01B5ALyYbzrXTGtc7LqRPGCz
---
 tests/test_dist_render.cpp | 18 ++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/tests/test_dist_render.cpp b/tests/test_dist_render.cpp
index 25041e1..38cca47 100644
--- a/tests/test_dist_render.cpp
+++ b/tests/test_dist_render.cpp
@@ -73,8 +73,21 @@ TEST(DistRender, PullSchedulerMatchesWholeFrame) {
         WorkerConfig wc;
         wc.host = "127.0.0.1"; wc.port = port; wc.width = W; wc.height = H;
         wc.verbose = false;
+        // retry_secs, as in the two tests below. The master binds on its own
+        // thread and the workers start immediately, so on a loaded machine a
+        // worker can reach connect() before the listener exists. With no
+        // retry that connect fails, run() returns an error the test then
+        // DISCARDS, the worker exits - and the master sits in accept()
+        // waiting for a connection that will never come, so the final join()
+        // never returns. Caught under ctest -j16 on a 2-core box: hung 1 run
+        // in 6, gdb showing Master::run in TcpListener::accept with both
+        // worker threads already gone.
+        wc.retry_secs = 8;
         Worker w(wc, [] { return std::make_unique<CpuIrExecutor>(); });
-        (void)w.run();
+        // Not discarded: a worker that gives up used to leave the master
+        // blocked in accept(), which reads as a 60 s ctest timeout rather
+        // than as the connect failure it is.
+        EXPECT_TRUE(w.run().has_value());
     };
     std::thread w1(run_worker), w2(run_worker);
 
@@ -122,8 +135,9 @@ TEST(DistRender, PushSchedulerCoversFrame) {
     std::thread mt([&] { mres = master.run(); });
     auto run_worker = [&] {
         WorkerConfig wc; wc.port = port; wc.width = W; wc.height = H; wc.verbose = false;
+        wc.retry_secs = 8;   // the startup race - see the note above
         Worker w(wc, [] { return std::make_unique<CpuIrExecutor>(); });
-        (void)w.run();
+        EXPECT_TRUE(w.run().has_value());
     };
     std::thread w1(run_worker), w2(run_worker);
     w1.join(); w2.join(); mt.join();
